The code evaluates `erfc` in double-double arithmetic with an absolute error bound `err` and returns
`h + (l - err)` when it rounds to the same double as `h + (l + err)`.  Below 0 the fast path is
`1 + erf (-x)` and up to `0x1.713786d9c7c09p+1` it is `1 - erf x`, with `erf` from a polynomial on
each of the intervals `[i/16, (i + 1)/16)` and an odd polynomial below `1/16`.  Above, it is
`exp (-x²) p (1/x)` with `p` a polynomial in `1/x²` on six intervals of `1/x` and `exp` from the
tables `2 ^ (i/64)` and a polynomial.  When the test fails, an accurate path evaluates the same
forms to about 104 bits with polynomials of higher degree on intervals of width `1/8` and on ten
intervals of `1/x`, and tables of 68 inputs, and one subnormal result, that even that path cannot
round supply their results.  Every `__builtin_fma` calls `Library.Numerics.Arithmetic.Fma.fma`, the correctly rounded fused
multiply-add, so the port computes CORE-MATH's results bit for bit.

The port follows the C code operation for operation, with these changes.  A C function that returns
results through pointers returns a tuple.  The tables are words in seven arrays, which `erfc` passes
to `erfcWith`, and their docstrings give the offsets of the C arrays; the short arrays `c0`, `p`,
`Q_1`, and `threshold` become constants in the code.  The C integers `i` and `k` that select or
count are integer-valued doubles or words: `k` of `exp_1` is a two's complement word with `sar` for
C's arithmetic shift, and `k` of `exp_accurate` an integer-valued double.  `roundeven_finite` adds
and subtracts `2 ^ 52`, which rounds ties to even, and `floor` follows from it.  `ldexp` multiplies
by powers of two that are doubles, which rounds once as C's `ldexp` does for the exponents that
occur.  The searches of the threshold arrays become chains of comparisons, and the scans of the
tables of hard cases are loops over all rows, with the same results since their inputs are distinct.
The dialect has no floating-point flags and no `errno`, so the tests and assignments of the
underflow flag and of `errno` are gone, and the expressions that C evaluates for their flags keep
their values.  Each constant is the bit pattern of its value, with the C literal in a comment.  The
`verified_compile` call at the end of the module checks the encoding one function at a time, and
the module's build needs about 2.7 GB.  `ErfcAccuracy.lean` checks the results against `ErfReference`. -/

